    "summary": "High‑energy Utica show with scorching China>Rider and relentless Truckin’ jam",
    "blurb": "The set delivers a blistering China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider segue, a soaring Here Comes Sunshine, and an extended Truckin' → The Other One → Eyes Of The World sequence. Jerry’s guitar soars while Phil’s bass drives the jams, and Keith adds melodic depth. Two excellent soundboard sources capture the performance with crystal‑clear fidelity.",
    "review": "March 22, 1973 at Utica Memorial Auditorium finds the Dead in top form, rolling from a relaxed first set into a high‑octane second that still feels effortlessly cohesive. Highlights include a razor‑sharp China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider transition that showcases Jerry’s scorching slide work and improvisational noodling, followed by a radiant Here Comes Sunshine that lifts the entire band. The centerpiece is the Truckin' → Nobody's Fault But Mine Jam → The Other One → Eyes Of The World stretch, where Phil’s “bass bombs” drive an unrelenting groove and Bill’s double‑time bursts keep the momentum fierce. Bird Song and Box of Rain add melodic balance, while Sugaree closes the first set on a sweet note. Two soundboard recordings—most notably the Sacks capture—present the show with pristine clarity, making this Utica performance a must‑listen for anyone chasing that 1973 magic.",
